      "what_must_be_true": "AI datacenter scaling must continue to demand massive increases in leading-edge transistor density, advanced packaging (HBM and 3D chiplet stacking), and materials engineering innovations, driving WFE growth and expanding AMAT's share and gross margins."
    },
    "preview_note": "Applied Materials heads into the print with exceptional fundamental momentum driven by the accelerating AI infrastructure build-out. Key focus areas for the quarter include: (1) leading-edge Foundry/Logic revenue strength as customers transition to Gate-All-Around (GAA) architectures, (2) DRAM and HBM packaging demand surging on high-bandwidth memory capacity additions, and (3) gross margin expansion via value-based pricing and operational leverage. With customers addressing fab space constraints and expanding cleanroom deliveries, strong execution on tool shipments and robust AGS service attach rates will determine whether AMAT can continue outperforming overall WFE growth.",
    "reasoning": "AMAT's Q3 FY2026 print demonstrates remarkable strength across Semiconductor Systems and Applied Global Services, benefiting from AI datacenter compute demand. Record revenue of $9.115B and non-GAAP gross margin of 50.4% reflect pricing power in critical materials engineering and advanced packaging technologies. Strong operating leverage drove operating margin to 34.0% and non-GAAP diluted EPS to $3.50. Looking forward, management guidance for Q4 FY26 of $10.25B in revenue and $4.02 in non-GAAP EPS underscores multi-year tailwinds in GAA logic, advanced DRAM/HBM, and 3D chiplet integration, supported by manufacturing capacity doubling by 2028."
  },
